const body = `<h1>4.4 — Attack: Close-Range Contact Damage</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The enemy can find you, and now it can reach you. "Touch equals minus one health" sounds
    like the easiest line in the unit. It is not, because the callback that detects the touch
    runs <em>sixty times a second</em> — and so does the player's death rule from
    <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>. Today you make contact hurt at a rate a human can survive, and
    you choose which of three hazard models does the work.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I stop a contact hit hurting me sixty times a second?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('4.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: Touching You Is Too Cheap</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Your goal: standing against the enemy should cost health, at a rate the player
        can react to.</strong> You have already built one hazard in this project and chosen how it
        would hurt you. Read those three options again — they are the same three, but the object
        wearing them has changed: this one <em>moves</em>. And a moving hazard can be solid or a
        trigger, so the third model below appears twice, once for each collider you could have
        given the enemy in 4.1. <strong>Three models, four rows</strong> — and the price column is
        the one that decides it:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Hazard model</th><th>What fires</th><th>What the player feels</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A solid enemy, touched once</strong></td>
            <td><code>OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)</code></td>
            <td>You bump into it and lose health once per bump.</td>
            <td>Damage lands on the frame the two shapes meet and never again while they stay in contact — one hit per <em>pass</em>, not one per frame you lean on it, which is how the coin counted a pickup in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>. The cost is the exploit: an enemy can pin you against a wall and you take nothing further, so the safest place in the fight becomes the inside of the enemy. A solid enemy also shoves your Dynamic player around, which is either the fight you designed in 4.1 or a fight you did not.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A trigger enemy, passed through once</strong></td>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></td>
            <td>It walks through you and one hit lands as it passes.</td>
            <td>A trigger is a sensor, not a wall: it reports what already happened and never shoves anybody — the same distinction as the coin versus the spike in 2.2. Buying that costs you the enemy's solidity: it passes through your walls unless you handle that separately, and a chaser that catches you and stops on top of you deals damage exactly once and then nothing at all, which is the wrong answer for a hunter.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A drain while contact lasts, with a cooldown</strong><br><em>solid flavour</em></td>
            <td><code>OnCollisionStay2D(Collision2D collision)</code></td>
            <td>It holds you against itself and your health drains at a steady, readable rate for as long as you stay there.</td>
            <td>The <code>Stay</code> callbacks fire on every physics step — sixty times a second. With no rate limit your entire health bar is gone in about a third of a second, because <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>'s death rule is only a comparison against zero. This is the one model that needs the cooldown from <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a>, and building that cooldown is the whole of today. It also keeps the shove: a solid enemy physically decides where the fight happens, and a player pinned in a corner is now being punished for it instead of spared.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A drain while contact lasts, with a cooldown</strong><br><em>trigger flavour</em></td>
            <td><code>OnTriggerStay2D(Collider2D other)</code></td>
            <td>It overlaps you and your health drains at the same steady rate for as long as it stays inside you.</td>
            <td>Identical every-frame cost and the identical fix: the cooldown. What the trigger buys you is a fight that stays wherever the player stands — nothing is shoved, and the enemy passes through walls — which is either the hunter you designed in 4.1 or a haunting. <em>Is Trigger</em> has to be ticked to match this callback name, or the Console stays empty.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The parameter types differ, and it changes what you can write.</strong>
        <code>OnTrigger*</code> hands you a <code>Collider2D</code>, which has <code>.gameObject</code>
        and <code>.CompareTag</code> directly. <code>OnCollision*</code> hands you a
        <code>Collision2D</code> — a data container with contact points and
        <code>relativeVelocity</code> — so the tag check becomes
        <code>collision.gameObject.CompareTag(...)</code>. One extra hop, and one extra chance to
        write it wrong. The checkbox and the callback name have to agree: <em>Is Trigger</em>
        ticked gives you the <code>OnTrigger*</code> callback, unticked gives you the
        <code>OnCollision*</code> one, and an unticked collider with a trigger callback in your
        file is not an error — it is silence.
    </div>

    <p>
        Whichever model you choose, the enemy still has to reach the player's health. That is a
        second decision, and there is more than one honest answer:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>How the enemy reaches the player's health</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>From the thing it touched</strong></td>
            <td>Ask the collided object for the <code>PlayerController</code> component.</td>
            <td>No wiring at all, and it works on every copy of the prefab. The lookup only happens on the frames a contact occurs, which is exactly when you can afford it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Up the hierarchy from the thing it touched</strong></td>
            <td>Ask the collided object's <em>parents</em> for the <code>PlayerController</code>.</td>
            <td>Survives a collider that lives on a child object — which is your situation if you put the player's collider on <em>Sprite</em> in 2.1. Costs a slightly longer search, and it can find a component you did not expect further up.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A <code>[SerializeField]</code> reference on each enemy</strong></td>
            <td>Drag the Player into a slot in the Inspector.</td>
            <td>Zero lookup cost and visible on the Inspector. But there are four enemies in the Overworld and each needs wiring — and it breaks the day the player is created at runtime instead of placed in the scene.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The every-frame problem, one more time.</strong> In <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a> the
        turret fired once per frame because a condition was true for sixty frames in a row. The
        <code>Stay</code> callbacks are the same disease. This is not a coincidence and it is not
        a Unity quirk — it is what "run this while the condition holds" means in every engine.
        The cure is the same too: a cooldown gate. Read the next table for the API, then build it.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Choosing a Hazard Model</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the four rows in the table did you choose, and what does the player learn to do about it after two encounters?</li>
<li>If you chose <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code> alone, what happens when a chasing enemy pins you against a wall? Is that a fair fight, or an exploit?</li>
<li>You built a hazard once already, in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>. What is the difference between that hazard and this one — and does a <em>moving</em> hazard change which model is correct?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

