<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: The API for a Contact Hit</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        This is the level-3 substitute for a walkthrough. The API is laid out below and the code
        is not: you write the file. Between them, these rows are the whole job — a callback, a
        tag check, a lookup, a call, a rate limit and three declarations. Every one of them costs
        something, so name the cost before you pick; otherwise you are not choosing, you are
        guessing.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Signature</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code><br><code>void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)</code> — a callback. Unity calls it; you never do.</td>
            <td>Fires on the first frame two solid colliders meet, with a body on at least one of the pair — the rule from 2.1, and the player has carried a Dynamic body since 2.2. The parameter is the <em>other</em> object's collision.</td>
            <td>Once per entry, and only once: the shapes have to separate and meet again for a second message, so an enemy that pins you deals nothing further. Misspelling the name is not an error, it is silence.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code><br><code>void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code> — a callback</td>
            <td>Fires on the first frame two shapes overlap, with <em>Is Trigger</em> ticked on at least one of them. The parameter is the <em>other</em> object's collider.</td>
            <td>One message per pass, not one per frame of leaning — a sensor that reports and never shoves. It also means the enemy is not a wall any more, which is a decision you made about its collider in 4.1.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnCollisionStay2D</code><br><code>void OnCollisionStay2D(Collision2D collision)</code> — a callback</td>
            <td>A message on every physics step the two solid colliders remain in contact — sixty of them a second.</td>
            <td>The message does not mean "a hit happened", it means "the condition is still true". Without the rate limit below, your death rule from 2.2 resolves the encounter in about a third of a second. A Kinematic body reports nothing against a static wall — it only collides with Dynamic bodies, which is what your player is.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerStay2D</code><br><code>void OnTriggerStay2D(Collider2D other)</code> — a callback</td>
            <td>The same every-physics-step message for two shapes that stay overlapped, with <em>Is Trigger</em> ticked.</td>
            <td>Identical every-frame cost, and the same cooldown is the answer. One extra thing to trip over: the tag check inside it runs sixty times a second too, so put the cheap check first and the expensive work last.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>CompareTag</code><br><code>bool CompareTag(string tag)</code>, asked of the object that carries the collider — <code>other.gameObject.CompareTag(...)</code> or <code>collision.gameObject.CompareTag(...)</code></td>
            <td>Asks the engine whether that object carries the tag, so one check tells you what <em>kind</em> of thing you touched.</td>
            <td>Exact spelling, and the tag must already exist in the project. It has to be asked of the object you touched, never of the script the callback lives on. And it tells you nothing about what that object can <em>do</em> — that is the next two rows.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code> / <code>GetComponentInParent&lt;T&gt;()</code><br><code>T GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code>, called on a GameObject or a component</td>
            <td>The component of that type on the object you call it on — here, the <code>PlayerController</code> that owns the health. The <code>InParent</code> form keeps walking up the parent chain to find it.</td>
            <td>Both are searches, and both hand you <code>null</code> when the type is not there. The plain version does not look at children, so the component has to sit on the object you actually touched; reach for <code>InParent</code> only if your player's collider is on a child. <code>InChildren</code> exists too and is the slowest of the three.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>A call on another script's method<br><code>TakeDamage(int amount)</code> — the method you wrote in 2.2, called on the reference you just looked up</td>
            <td>Hands the problem to the object that owns the data: the enemy never touches the health number, it only asks. The same method serves the spike, the bullet and any healing you add later, because a signed amount can travel both ways.</td>
            <td>You declared it <code>private</code> in 2.2, and 3.4 is where it was widened so another script could reach it — if your 3.4 hit was detected on <code>PlayerController</code> instead of on the bullet, that never happened and widening it is part of today's work. Read your own method before you pass a number: it <em>subtracts</em>, so the positive amount is the one that hurts, and a sign that is backwards is not a compiler error — it is a heal.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] private int damage</code><br>a field declaration, not a call — class body, never inside a method</td>
            <td>A named value that survives every frame and appears in the Inspector as the designer's knob for how punishing one contact is.</td>
            <td>The type has to match the parameter of the method you call: <code>TakeDamage(int amount)</code> takes an <code>int</code>, so a <code>float</code> knob cannot be passed without a conversion the compiler refuses. And because 2.2's method also holds the death rule, your default is a ratio against the player's maximum health from 2.3 — not a number you happen to like.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>The cooldown pair<br>two fields in the class body: one serialized interval, one private remembered moment</td>
            <td>The rate limit from 3.3, pointed at a callback instead of at <code>Update()</code>: the interval is how often this enemy may hurt the player, the remembered moment is when it last did.</td>
            <td>Both fields belong in the class body — declared inside the callback, the remembered moment is rebuilt on every call and remembers nothing, which is the same scope rule as 1.3 and looks exactly like a working rate limit until you count the hits. Seed the moment one interval <em>before</em> the scene starts, the way 3.3 seeded its turret, so the first contact hurts immediately instead of serving a penalty for a hit that never happened. And one interval means the second hit inside the window is <em>discarded</em>, not queued: nothing is owed to the player when the window closes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Time.time</code><br><code>float Time.time</code></td>
            <td>The seconds since the scene loaded, counting up — the clock your gate is compared against.</td>
            <td>A moment means nothing on its own; it is only meaningful against another moment on the same clock. If your remembered value is a countdown decremented by <code>Time.deltaTime</code>, that is a different row of 3.3's table and it cannot be compared against this clock.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Debug.Log(object)</code><br><code>void Debug.Log(object message)</code></td>
            <td>Prints to the Console — the only feedback this game has until Unit 5.</td>
            <td>Text only, and it prints when <em>you</em> call it. Print the moment damage lands and you can count hits per second against your cooldown knob, which is the only way to prove a rate limit works with no UI at all.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Four facts a signature cannot carry.</strong> First, the callback is silent about
        your mistakes: forget the collider, mistype the method name, or tick the wrong checkbox,
        and the Console stays empty — "no error at all" is the hardest bug in this unit to find,
        which is why you check the component before you check the code. Second, the tag check is
        the first filter and the component lookup is the second, and a lookup that finds nothing
        hands you <code>null</code> rather than stopping the script, so every line below it has
        to be written as though the lookup succeeded. Third, the player's health lives on
        <code>PlayerController</code> and nowhere else — that is where you put it in
        <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>. Fourth, the callback knows nothing about your state machine:
        it fires on <em>any</em> contact between the two colliders, including the frame a calmly
        patrolling guard walks into you. If damage should only land while the enemy is chasing,
        that condition is yours to write, and 4.2's <code>Attack</code> trigger is waiting on the
        same decision.
    </div>
</div>

