            <li>What guard check protects you if the object carries no <code>PlayerController</code>? What does the error look like without it?</li>
            <li>Read the line in your own method that changes health, and work out which sign <em>hurts</em>. Name it, then say out loud what the other sign would do to the player instead.</li>
            <li>Is the method callable from <code>EnemyController.cs</code> yet? If your 3.4 damage was delivered by <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>'s bullet, it is public already; if it was delivered on the player's side, it may still be private and that is part of today.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: what does NOT happen.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Is anything destroyed here? Compare this callback to the projectile's in <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a> and explain the difference in one sentence.</li>
            <li>Should the enemy stop moving while it attacks, or keep chasing? What does each look like to the player, and what does your animator's <code>Attack</code> trigger do about it? (See <a href="4.2.html">4.2</a>.)</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Proving it:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What do you print each time damage lands, so you can <em>count</em> hits per second against your cooldown knob?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Do not reach for a manager.</strong> <code>StatManager.cs</code> is still an empty
        stub with no health in it and no method to change anything. Health lives on
        <code>PlayerController</code>, where you put it in 2.2, and that is what you call. The
        manager earns its place in 5.3, when a scene change finally gives you a reason.
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and play. Walk into an enemy and hold still. You should take one hit, then one more
        every cooldown interval — and when your health reaches zero, your death rule from 2.2
        should fire exactly as it did for the hazard you built then.
    </p>

</div>

